    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll implement the countersign action. Let me start by reading the existing code to understand the structure.\n\nNow let me check the demo_identity.go file to understand the authentication:\n\nNow I understand the structure. Let me implement the countersign action:\n\n1. Add `countersignOp` to `operations.go`\n2. Update the `New` function in `approval.go` to register the countersign route\n\nLet me first update the operations file:\n\nNow update the `approval.go` to register the countersign route:\n\nNow let's build and test:\n\nLet me verify the changes by reading the modified files:\n\nThe implementation is complete. Let me verify the schema was already updated for countersign:\n\nThe schema already includes `countersign.v6`. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded the `countersign` action:\n\n1. **`operations.go`**: Declared `countersignOp` with:\n   - Name: `countersign.v6`\n   - Role: `approver`\n   - From: `pending-second`, To: `approved`\n   - `Confirm: true` to require amount validation\n   - `Field: \"\"` for single-item response format\n   - `Event: action.NoEvent` (no outbox event)\n\n2. **`approval.go`**: Registered the route using `protocol.Confirm`:\n   - Path: `/purchases/{id}/countersign`\n   - Handler validates `{\"amount\":N}` body\n   - Nested in middleware chain: `Confirm` → `Item` (reopen) → `HTTP` (batch routes)\n\nThe shared `protocol.Confirm` adapter handles authentication, role check, request body validation (amount in 1..2147483647), Idempotency-Key validation, and binds demo identity. The action kernel's `Executor.Run` handles all idempotency, concurrency, eligibility checks (pending-second status, different creator/approver, amount matching), transactional writes to ActionRequest and ApprovalAudit."
